  "input": {
    "rationale": "The run's two biggest findings share a mechanism - the blocking send on the FSM mutation channel - so the durable distinction between them is worth stating once, with both sides' evidence, rather than leaving a reader to reconstruct it from separate interpretations: at startup the send is issued before any consumer exists and before NewRaft has returned a handle, which makes it fatal; in the running path the consumer exists and the same select observes the shutdown channel, which a caller can close, and the measured effect of a slow FSM is a per-batch delay rather than a stop.",
    "sources": [],
    "investigations": [],
    "knowledge": [
      {
        "id": null,
        "ref": "@fsm_channel_startup_vs_runtime",
        "kind": "fact",
        "statement": "The same blocking send on fsmMutateCh has different consequences at startup and at runtime. In the startup replay the constructor issues it before NewRaft has started the FSM goroutine and before it has returned a handle, so nothing drains the channel and no participant can close the shutdown channel the send also selects on; once the replay needs more batches than the channel holds, the constructor cannot complete. In the running path the FSM goroutine consumes batches and a caller holding the handle can close the shutdown channel, so a slow FSM delays the main loop by about one batch at a time - measured as a backlog that stayed within a single entry behind a 25ms Apply, with and without a buffered apply channel - instead of stopping it.",
        "conditions": "Startup side: RestoreCommittedLogs enabled with a CommitTrackingLogStore, and a replay window above 128 batches of the configured MaxAppendEntries. Runtime side: the measured configuration is a single-voter in-memory cluster with a 25ms Apply and 400 applies fired without waiting.",
